+## 9. Known limitations and unsupported cases
+
+- Catch2 does not roll back test-owned or external state: globals, statics,
+  files, environment variables, databases, network services, child processes,
+  or threads can carry effects into a retry. Tests must manage such state.
+- A fatal signal, structured exception, stack overflow, forced termination,
+  or process crash cannot be recovered and retried. Catch2 only performs
+  best-effort balanced reporting before the existing termination behavior.
+- Retries are sequential. There is no delay/backoff, parallel retry
+  scheduling, persistence across process launches, or retry-only filter.
+- The unit of retry is the whole logical test case. Individual assertions,
+  section paths, generator values, and partial runs are not retried alone.
+- TAP buffers its own assertion lines while retries are enabled. Output written
+  directly by the test can therefore interleave differently, although TAP's
+  reporter text remains unchanged when no retry occurs.
+- The accepted retry count is an unsigned integer. Very large values are
+  represented safely, but an always-failing test can naturally take a very
+  long time if given a very large retry budget.
